<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
    LOTAR Core vocabulary for SRMD — Common part (shared by the P5xx domain parts; currently P520 —
    P515's content was integrated into P520 on 2026-09-05, D-23).
    See LOTAR_Core_Common_Vocabulary.md for the full attribute table and design notes.

    Modeled on SRMDMICCore.xsd / SRMDSICCore.xsd: this file defines no new elements itself. It
    xs:includes the base SRMD structural schema (SRMD.xsd, already in this project) and adds
    Schematron rules that constrain the generic SRMD Classification/ClassificationEntry
    key-value-pair model to the fixed keyword set proposed under
    Classification[@type='org.lotar-international.lotar-core.common'].

    Scope: only the administrative/legal cluster of GeneralPLMInfoType not already covered by
    MIC Core (see MIC_Core_to_LOTAR_Mapping.md and P515_P520_vs_MIC_Core_Gap.md). Deliberately
    excludes Traceability_Link_Type and anything built from it — inter-model/inter-artifact
    linking is deferred to a future P550 standard. SIC Core was not consulted for this vocabulary
    per direction.

    This is a first-draft proposal: unlike SRMDMICCore.xsd / SRMDSICCore.xsd it does not yet
    include Schematron Quick Fixes (sqf:) for auto-remediation — flagged here as follow-up work
    once the vocabulary itself is reviewed and stable.

    2026-07-20 update (confirmed, later same day): SIC Core is disregarded for the LOTAR Core
    mandatory baseline for now — the administrative/legal ground this vocabulary and MIC Core
    already cover is treated as sufficient; SRMDSICCore.xsd remains in WaitForLater/ and is not
    part of the mandatory schema set. See LOTAR_Core_Common_Vocabulary.md.

    2026-07-20 update: added administrative.generation-tool and
    administrative.variable-naming-convention, both mandatory, migrated from FMI 3.0.2's root
    provenance attributes (@generationTool, @variableNamingConvention) per the decision that the
    FMI3 provenance layer now lives here instead of being checked against FMU files directly (see
    FMISchematron/deprecated/lotar-fmi3.0.2-provenance.sch's own deprecation note). Export-control
    fields (legal.export-controls / legal.export-control-marking / legal.special-markings) were
    already mandatory here — confirmed by the same decision, not changed.

    2026-07-20 (same day, follow-up): the FMI 2.0.5 provenance layer
    (FMISchematron/deprecated/lotar-fmi2.0.5-provenance.sch) was deprecated the same way and
    migrated here too — no new keywords needed, it reuses the same two new keywords above plus
    five already-existing ones. FMI2's @numberOfEventIndicators (its one attribute with no FMI3
    analog) was deliberately NOT
    migrated — see LOTAR_Core_Common_Vocabulary.md for why.

    2026-07-20 (later same day, item 4 of the profile-opportunity closure pass): added
    administrative.disposal-reason, Mandatory but conditional on a Retracted STC life-cycle entry
    existing anywhere in the SRMD instance — closes the PD12 (Disposal) profile opportunity
    flagged in FMI_SSP_Evaluation_vs_Requirements_v3_LOTARCore.md §4. Added to the
    allowed-keywords whitelist below and enforced via a new conditional sch:assert (pattern
    "LOTAR Core Common: Conditional Mandatory Attributes"), mirroring the P515-ARCH pattern of
    conditional "required if X exists" asserts already used in
    FMISchematron/lotar-p515-archival.sch. See LOTAR_Core_Common_Vocabulary.md for the full
    rationale.

    2026-07-20 (later same day, PD4 Tier 1 closure): added administrative.size-bytes, unconditionally
    Mandatory, recording the size in bytes of the whole Digital Object this SRMD instance describes
    (the entire zipped FMU, or the entire zipped SSP, whichever container this SRMD is embedded in
    or is sidecar to) — PD4's own text asks for "the size of the file recorded as an integer, in
    bytes" for each Digital Object, and this is the archive-level ("Tier 1") half of a three-tier
    proposal; Tier 2 (per-contained-artifact size inside an SSP) is enforced separately on
    stc:Resource in lotar-stc-archival.sch, using the same keyword name for consistency. A Tier 3
    (per-file-inside-an-FMU-zip manifest) was proposed but confirmed out of scope (2026-07-20) —
    judged redundant/overkill given Tiers 1+2 already satisfy PD4's per-Digital-Object requirement.
    administrative.size-bytes deliberately reuses P515_SCHEMA.xsd's existing @sizeBytes naming
    convention (FMISchematron/lotar-p515-archival.sch, P515-ARCH-100c/101a) even though it is a
    Classification keyword rather than a native XSD attribute (SRMD.xsd's root has no size/length
    attribute and no xs:anyAttribute wildcard to extend via Schematron, the same constraint already
    documented for administrative.disposal-reason and administrative.generation-tool above).

    2026-07-21 (P6 closure): added administrative.submission-agreement-ref, unconditionally
    Mandatory — closes P6 (Submission Agreement), whose text requires SIPs to "contain information
    that reference the Submission Agreement between Producer and Archive," citing METS
    metsHdr/altRecordID as the example mechanism. This keyword is the SRMD-Classification-level
    equivalent of altRecordID: a repeatable-in-spirit (single-value-per-entry, as with every other
    Common keyword) reference to the Submission Agreement of record, populated with an identifier
    or a URI (via ClassificationEntry's optional xlink:href, same convention already used for
    Copyright/License/Export_Controls). See METS's own definition of altRecordID for the pattern
    this keyword mirrors:
    https://mets.github.io/METS_Docs/mets_xsd_Complex_Type_metsType.html#metsType_metsHdr_altRecordID
    ("The alternative record identifier element <altRecordID> allows one to use alternative record
    identifier values for the digital object represented by the METS document; the primary record
    identifier is stored in the OBJID attribute in the root <mets> element.") Made unconditionally
    Mandatory (not conditional, unlike administrative.disposal-reason) because P6's own text is an
    unconditional SHALL — every SIP is assumed to be covered by some Submission Agreement with the
    Archive, per OAIS's own foundational producer-archive relationship.

    2026-07-21 (provenance unification, later same day): per explicit direction to unify FMI2/FMI3/
    SSP provenance into a single mechanism (this file + SRMDMICCore.xsd) rather than maintain
    parallel, differently-tiered checks per format, and to make the basic "description" field
    Mandatory in that unified mechanism:

      - administrative.variable-naming-convention REMOVED (both from the Mandatory pattern below
        and from the allowed-keywords whitelist). Confirmed by direction: this is a technical/
        structural flag about FMI's own ModelVariables naming scheme (flat vs. structured), not
        authorship/legal/provenance metadata — it does not belong in this vocabulary regardless of
        format. Restored instead as a native rule directly in lotar-fmi2.0.5-archival.sch /
        lotar-fmi3.0.2-archival.sch (see those files' own headers). administrative.generation-tool
        is NOT affected by this — it stays here, since "what tool generated this" is genuinely
        provenance, not a structural flag.
      - administrative.created-by PROMOTED from the "Recommended (Optional) Attributes" pattern to
        the "Mandatory Attributes" pattern below. This closes the gap flagged in
        FMISchematron/FMI_vs_SSP_LOTAR_Rule_Comparison.md §1a: SRMD's own native @author attribute
        had an independent [M] mandate (SRMD-ARCH-102 in lotar-srmd-archival.sch) while this
        Classification-layer equivalent was only Recommended — two differently-tiered mechanisms
        for the same fact. SRMD-ARCH-102 has been retired (see lotar-srmd-archival.sch's own
        header) in favor of this single Classification-layer mandate, which now applies uniformly
        to FMI2/FMI3/SSP-wrapped SRMD alike (this file has no format-specific logic at all).
      - A new Mandatory check for MIC Core's purpose-objectives.model (the closest existing
        keyword to a basic "description" field) was added below (see the new "LOTAR Core (Common)
        override: MIC Core Mandatory Additions" pattern), per explicit direction to make the basic
        description Mandatory. purpose-objectives.model is defined in the vendored, do-not-edit
        SRMDMICCore.xsd, where it sits at Recommended tier only (role="info") — same "two
        mechanisms, two tiers" problem as administrative.created-by above, but on a keyword LOTAR
        doesn't own. Rather than edit the vendored external MIC Core schema (against this project's
        established do-not-edit-vendored-files convention — see SRMD.xsd/P515_SCHEMA.xsd/the FMI
        and SSP XSDs for the same precedent), this LOTAR-authored file adds its OWN stricter check
        against the MIC-Core-scoped Classification block instead, the same "layer a stricter LOTAR
        mandate on top of an unmodified vendored schema" pattern already used throughout this
        project. This closes the other half of the SRMD-ARCH-101 retirement (see
        lotar-srmd-archival.sch): purpose-objectives.model is the Classification-layer equivalent
        of native @description, and is now Mandatory here too.
      - administrative.version (already Mandatory, unchanged) is now documented as the shared,
        unified home for THREE previously-separate content-versioning fields: SSP's
        SystemStructureDescription/@fileversion (migrated 2026-07-21, see
        FMISchematron/ssp2.0.1-rule-inventory.md §1), and — as of this same revision — FMI's own
        fmiModelDescription/@version (FMI2 and FMI3 alike). The native FMI @version rule
        (FMI2-ARCH-010i / FMI3-ARCH-010i, formerly [R] directly in each archival.sch) has been
        retired the same way SRMD-ARCH-101/102 was — see both lotar-fmi2.0.5-archival.sch and
        lotar-fmi3.0.2-archival.sch's own headers. One keyword, one tier, three formats.

    BUG FOUND AND FIXED (2026-07-21, same revision): every sch:rule/@context attribute in this file
    that referenced a sch:let-declared variable (e.g. context="...[@type=$lotar-core-common-type]")
    failed to compile under lxml's isoschematron (libxslt): "xsltCompilePattern: failed to compile"
    — confirmed via a minimal reproduction that sch:let variables work fine inside assert/report
    test= expressions but are NOT supported inside a rule's context= match pattern by this
    toolchain, regardless of whether the let is pattern-scoped or schema-scoped. This means this
    file's embedded Schematron had never actually been compiled/validated before this revision (it
    was authored and reasoned about, but never run). Fixed by inlining the literal
    Classification/@type string directly into every context= attribute instead of referencing the
    variable there; the one remaining $lotar-core-common-type reference (inside the "Classification
    Presence" pattern's assert test=) is unaffected and left as-is, since variables in test=
    expressions do work. The identical anti-pattern (variable used in context=) also exists in
    SRMDLOTARCore_DublinCore.xsd, SRMDLOTARCore_P515.xsd, and SRMDLOTARCore_P520.xsd — NOT fixed
    (2026-09-05 update: _P520.xsd IS now fixed and verified under lxml, D-23; _P515.xsd retired) —
    here (out of scope for this revision, which only touches Common); flagged in
    FMISchematron/FMI_vs_SSP_LOTAR_Rule_Comparison.md and the artifacts/decisions log as a
    follow-up needed. SRMDMICCore.xsd has the same pattern too but is vendored/do-not-edit (see its
    own header) — if it's genuinely broken the same way, that's an upstream MIC Core issue, not
    something to patch here.

    2026-08-04 (T-04, risk-profile plan execution): added risk.level and
    administrative.archiving-required to the allowed-keywords whitelist, plus a new "Value
    Validity" pattern checking both are one of their closed-enum values wherever they appear. Both
    are new LOTAR-native keywords (no GeneralPLMInfoType analogue) whose PRIMARY, Mandatory
    attachment point is not a generic SRMD root's Classification block at all - it is a specific
    Output particle inside a DTMD.xsd instance (DefinitionPhase/DefineResultQuality/Output for
    risk.level; FulfillmentPhase/DecideObjectiveFulfillment/Output for
    administrative.archiving-required - see LOTAR_Risk_Assessment_Method.md Sec 4). This file's
    own sch:rule contexts are hard-anchored to /srmd:SimulationResourceMetaData/... and cannot
    fire on a DTMD-hosted instance, so NOT added to the "Mandatory Attributes" pattern below -
    that would incorrectly require every generic SRMD to carry a risk level. Their real Mandatory
    enforcement, scoped to the DTMD attachment point, is task T-09a (build
    lotar-dtmd-archival.sch), which depends on DTMD.xsd being vendored first (T-08, still open).
    Added here only so the keywords are recognized (whitelist) and self-consistent (enum check)
    wherever they do appear, including an SRMD sidecar mirroring the DTMD's risk decision for
    cross-reference convenience.

    Net effect of this revision: "provenance" for FMI2, FMI3, and SSP now runs through exactly one
    mechanism — this file plus SRMDMICCore.xsd's Classification keywords, validated against any
    SRMD instance regardless of which format it's embedded in or sidecar to. No format-specific
    provenance Schematron exists anywhere in this project any more (lotar-fmi2.0.5-provenance.sch /
    lotar-fmi3.0.2-provenance.sch were already deprecated 2026-07-20; the newer SRMD-ARCH-101/102
    native-attribute duplicate is retired as of this same revision). Real-world tool compliance
    with provenance fields is thin regardless of format (see fmi2/fmi3 corpus reports — 49-94% of
    real files miss at least one provenance field), which is exactly why a single shared mechanism
    is preferable to maintaining several slightly-different ones: there's no format-specific reason
    for the bar to differ.

    2026-08-06 (Dublin Core absorption): the standalone SRMDLOTARCore_DublinCore.xsd (its own
    Classification type, 'org.lotar-international.lotar-core.dublin-core') is retired and its 7
    keywords — dc.contributor, dc.coverage, dc.language, dc.publisher, dc.relation, dc.source,
    dc.subject — are folded into THIS file's Classification type
    ('org.lotar-international.lotar-core.common') instead, at the same Recommended tier they
    already had. No keyword-name collision existed with any pre-existing Common keyword (checked
    against the allowed-keywords whitelist below before merging), so this is a pure consolidation,
    not a reconciliation of conflicting definitions. Rationale: the Dublin Core mapping was never
    architecturally independent — 8 of its 15 terms already reused Common/MIC-Core fields, and the
    remaining 7 were always going to co-reside in the same SRMD document as this file's own
    Classification block. Maintaining them as a second Classification type bought no isolation
    benefit (both are LOTAR-authored, both are mandatory-baseline vocabulary) and cost a second
    file, a second .sch to gate, and a second Classification-presence check that every consuming
    instance would otherwise need to satisfy. The relationship to Dublin Core Metadata Element Set
    1.1 is preserved as documentation only (see the "Dublin Core mapping (D2)" section of
    LOTAR_Core_Common_Vocabulary.md for the full 15-term table and rationale) — it is no longer a
    separate namespace/type in the schema itself. The old file is retained at
    deprecated/SRMDLOTARCore_DublinCore.xsd for historical reference, per this project's
    non-destructive convention; it is no longer part of the mandatory schema set and is not
    referenced by any current documentation. dc.contributor, dc.relation, dc.source, and
    dc.subject remain repeatable (ported verbatim from the old file's own repeatable-keywords
    rule, now folded into this file's "Attribute Rules" pattern below) — every other keyword in
    this file's whitelist remains single-valued-per-entry, as before.
-->
<xs:schema xmlns:xs="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema" elementFormDefault="qualified"
    xmlns:vc="http://www.w3.org/2007/XMLSchema-versioning" vc:minVersion="1.1"
    xmlns:srmd="http://ssp-standard.org/SSPTraceability1/SimulationResourceMetaData"
    xmlns:stc="http://ssp-standard.org/SSPTraceability1/SSPTraceabilityCommon"
    xmlns:ssc="http://ssp-standard.org/SSP1/SystemStructureCommon"
    xmlns:sch="http://purl.oclc.org/dsdl/schematron"
    targetNamespace="http://ssp-standard.org/SSPTraceability1/SimulationResourceMetaData">

    <xs:include schemaLocation="../schemas-vendored/SRMD.xsd"/>
    <!-- D-24 (2026-09-05): risk.level, model-validation.mode, signature.method and
         signature.validation-note are no longer LOTAR keywords. The credible-process layer owns them
         under Classification[@type='org.credible-process.core'] (credibility.level CL1..CL4 replaces
         risk.level R1..R4; credible-process-schemas/vocabulary/). LOTAR keeps
         administrative.archiving-required here and cites the layer's level. -->

    <xs:annotation>
        <xs:appinfo>
            <sch:title>LOTAR Core (Common) Classification Checks</sch:title>
            <sch:ns uri="http://ssp-standard.org/SSPTraceability1/SimulationResourceMetaData" prefix="srmd"/>
            <sch:ns uri="http://ssp-standard.org/SSPTraceability1/SSPTraceabilityCommon" prefix="stc"/>

            <sch:pattern>
                <sch:title>LOTAR Core Common: Classification Presence</sch:title>
                <sch:let name="lotar-core-common-type" value="'org.lotar-international.lotar-core.common'"/>
                <sch:rule context="/srmd:SimulationResourceMetaData" role="info">
                    <sch:assert test="count(stc:Classification[@type=$lotar-core-common-type])&lt;=1">
                        More than one LOTAR Core (Common) classification.
                    </sch:assert>
                </sch:rule>
            </sch:pattern>

            <sch:pattern>
                <sch:title>LOTAR Core Common: Mandatory Attributes</sch:title>
                <sch:rule context="/srmd:SimulationResourceMetaData/stc:Classification[@type='org.lotar-international.lotar-core.common']">
                    <sch:assert test="stc:ClassificationEntry[@keyword='administrative.version']">Missing mandatory Version attribute.</sch:assert>
                    <sch:assert test="stc:ClassificationEntry[@keyword='administrative.created-on']">Missing mandatory Created On attribute.</sch:assert>
                    <sch:assert test="stc:ClassificationEntry[@keyword='administrative.usage']">Missing mandatory Usage attribute.</sch:assert>
                    <sch:assert test="stc:ClassificationEntry[@keyword='administrative.model-dependency']">Missing mandatory Model Dependency attribute.</sch:assert>
                    <sch:assert test="stc:ClassificationEntry[@keyword='administrative.model-hierarchy']">Missing mandatory Model Hierarchy attribute.</sch:assert>
                    <sch:assert test="stc:ClassificationEntry[@keyword='administrative.abstraction-level']">Missing mandatory Abstraction Level attribute.</sch:assert>
                    <sch:assert test="stc:ClassificationEntry[@keyword='administrative.privacy-rules']">Missing mandatory Privacy Rules attribute.</sch:assert>
                    <sch:assert test="stc:ClassificationEntry[@keyword='administrative.specification-type-objectives']">Missing mandatory Specification Type Objectives attribute.</sch:assert>
                    <sch:assert test="stc:ClassificationEntry[@keyword='administrative.generation-tool']">Missing mandatory Generation Tool attribute.</sch:assert>
                    <sch:assert test="stc:ClassificationEntry[@keyword='administrative.created-by']">Missing mandatory Created By attribute. Promoted from Recommended 2026-07-21 as part of the FMI2/FMI3/SSP provenance unification (see header REVISION note) — this is the Classification-layer equivalent of native @author, previously enforced independently and less strictly than SRMD-ARCH-102 (now retired in favor of this single mandate).</sch:assert>
                    <sch:assert test="stc:ClassificationEntry[@keyword='administrative.size-bytes']">Missing mandatory Size (Bytes) attribute — required for every SRMD instance, recording the size in bytes of the whole Digital Object this instance describes (PD4).</sch:assert>
                    <sch:assert test="stc:ClassificationEntry[@keyword='administrative.submission-agreement-ref']">Missing mandatory Submission Agreement Reference attribute (P6) — every SIP must reference the Submission Agreement between Producer and Archive.</sch:assert>
                    <sch:assert test="stc:ClassificationEntry[@keyword='legal.copyright']">Missing mandatory Copyright attribute.</sch:assert>
                    <sch:assert test="stc:ClassificationEntry[@keyword='legal.license']">Missing mandatory License attribute.</sch:assert>
                    <sch:assert test="stc:ClassificationEntry[@keyword='legal.export-controls']">Missing mandatory Export Controls attribute.</sch:assert>
                    <sch:assert test="stc:ClassificationEntry[@keyword='legal.export-control-marking']">Missing mandatory Export Control Marking attribute.</sch:assert>
                    <sch:assert test="stc:ClassificationEntry[@keyword='legal.special-markings']">Missing mandatory Special Markings attribute.</sch:assert>
                </sch:rule>
            </sch:pattern>

            <sch:pattern>
                <sch:title>LOTAR Core (Common) override: MIC Core Mandatory Additions</sch:title>
                <!--
                    Added 2026-07-21 (provenance unification): purpose-objectives.model is MIC
                    Core's closest equivalent to a basic "description" field (SRMDMICCore.xsd's own
                    fix-message calls it "model purpose attribute"), and per explicit direction the
                    basic description must be Mandatory in the unified provenance mechanism. MIC
                    Core itself (SRMDMICCore.xsd, vendored from the external MIC-Core specification
                    — see that file's own header) keeps it at Recommended tier (role="info"); this
                    file does NOT edit that vendored schema (same do-not-edit-vendored convention as
                    SRMD.xsd/P515_SCHEMA.xsd elsewhere in this project) but instead layers its own
                    stricter check on top, targeting the MIC-Core-scoped Classification block
                    directly. This is the Classification-layer counterpart to the retired
                    SRMD-ARCH-101 native @description mandate (see lotar-srmd-archival.sch).
                -->
                <sch:rule context="/srmd:SimulationResourceMetaData/stc:Classification[@type='org.mic-core.mic-core']">
                    <sch:assert test="stc:ClassificationEntry[@keyword='purpose-objectives.model']">
                        Missing mandatory Purpose/Objectives (basic description) attribute
                        (purpose-objectives.model) — Recommended-only in the base MIC Core schema,
                        promoted to Mandatory here as the unified provenance layer's basic
                        description field for FMI2, FMI3, and SSP alike.
                    </sch:assert>
                </sch:rule>
            </sch:pattern>

            <sch:pattern>
                <sch:title>LOTAR Core Common: Conditional Mandatory Attributes</sch:title>
                <!--
                    Item 4 (2026-07-20): administrative.disposal-reason is Mandatory only when the
                    SRMD instance carries a Retracted STC life-cycle entry (anywhere in the
                    document — life-cycle information can occur at multiple phase/step levels, not
                    only at the Classification's own enclosing element). Mirrors the P515-ARCH
                    pattern of conditional "required if X exists" asserts in
                    FMISchematron/lotar-p515-archival.sch. Closes PD12 (Disposal), whose own text
                    requires "the name of the authorising agent and reasons for deletion" once a
                    disposal/retraction event exists — the authorising-agent half is covered
                    separately by lotar-stc-archival.sch's universal Signature mandate on the
                    Retracted entry itself.
                -->
                <sch:rule context="/srmd:SimulationResourceMetaData/stc:Classification[@type='org.lotar-international.lotar-core.common']">
                    <sch:assert test="not(//stc:Retracted) or stc:ClassificationEntry[@keyword='administrative.disposal-reason']">
                        Missing mandatory Disposal Reason attribute (administrative.disposal-reason) — required because this SRMD instance carries at least one Retracted life-cycle entry.
                    </sch:assert>
                </sch:rule>
            </sch:pattern>

            <sch:pattern>
                <sch:title>LOTAR Core Common: Recommended (Optional) Attributes</sch:title>
                <sch:rule context="/srmd:SimulationResourceMetaData/stc:Classification[@type='org.lotar-international.lotar-core.common']" role="info">
                    <sch:assert test="stc:ClassificationEntry[@keyword='administrative.last-modified-date']">Missing optional Last Modified Date attribute.</sch:assert>
                    <!-- administrative.created-by MOVED to the Mandatory pattern above 2026-07-21 (provenance unification) -->
                    <sch:assert test="stc:ClassificationEntry[@keyword='administrative.author-organization']">Missing optional Author Organization attribute.</sch:assert>
                    <sch:assert test="stc:ClassificationEntry[@keyword='administrative.approver']">Missing optional Approver attribute.</sch:assert>
                    <sch:assert test="stc:ClassificationEntry[@keyword='administrative.approval-state']">Missing optional Approval State attribute.</sch:assert>
                    <sch:assert test="stc:ClassificationEntry[@keyword='administrative.authorization']">Missing optional Authorization attribute.</sch:assert>
                </sch:rule>
            </sch:pattern>

            <sch:pattern>
                <sch:title>LOTAR Core Common: Dublin Core Recommended Attributes (absorbed 2026-08-06)</sch:title>
                <!--
                    Ported verbatim from the retired SRMDLOTARCore_DublinCore.xsd (see this file's
                    header note). These are the 7 of Dublin Core Metadata Element Set 1.1's 15 terms
                    that have no existing MIC Core / Common / structural counterpart (the other 8
                    are documented as equivalent to already-existing fields — see
                    LOTAR_Core_Common_Vocabulary.md's "Dublin Core mapping (D2)" section). Still
                    Recommended, not Mandatory, for the same reason as before: these DC roles are
                    not universally applicable to every archived simulation artifact.
                -->
                <sch:rule context="/srmd:SimulationResourceMetaData/stc:Classification[@type='org.lotar-international.lotar-core.common']" role="info">
                    <sch:assert test="stc:ClassificationEntry[@keyword='dc.contributor']">Missing optional Contributor attribute (Dublin Core "contributor").</sch:assert>
                    <sch:assert test="stc:ClassificationEntry[@keyword='dc.coverage']">Missing optional Coverage attribute (Dublin Core "coverage" — spatial/temporal scope).</sch:assert>
                    <sch:assert test="stc:ClassificationEntry[@keyword='dc.language']">Missing optional Language attribute (Dublin Core "language").</sch:assert>
                    <sch:assert test="stc:ClassificationEntry[@keyword='dc.publisher']">Missing optional Publisher attribute (Dublin Core "publisher" — the entity making the resource available, distinct from its creator).</sch:assert>
                    <sch:assert test="stc:ClassificationEntry[@keyword='dc.relation']">Missing optional Relation attribute (Dublin Core "relation" — a related resource).</sch:assert>
                    <sch:assert test="stc:ClassificationEntry[@keyword='dc.source']">Missing optional Source attribute (Dublin Core "source" — a related resource from which this one is derived).</sch:assert>
                    <sch:assert test="stc:ClassificationEntry[@keyword='dc.subject']">Missing optional Subject attribute (Dublin Core "subject" — topic/keywords).</sch:assert>
                </sch:rule>
            </sch:pattern>

            <sch:pattern>
                <sch:title>LOTAR Core Common: administrative.archiving-required Value Validity</sch:title>
                <!--
                    Added 2026-08-04 (T-04). Both keywords are closed-enum: risk.level must be one
                    of R1/R2/R3/R4 (D-7); administrative.archiving-required must be one of yes/no
                    (D-17). This checks value self-consistency wherever the keyword appears; it
                    does NOT check presence/mandatoriness, since that is DTMD-context-specific and
                    belongs to lotar-dtmd-archival.sch (T-09a), not this SRMD-scoped file.
                -->
                <sch:rule context="/srmd:SimulationResourceMetaData/stc:Classification[@type='org.lotar-international.lotar-core.common']/stc:ClassificationEntry[@keyword='administrative.archiving-required']">
                    <sch:assert test=". = 'yes' or . = 'no'">
                        Invalid administrative.archiving-required value '<sch:value-of select="."/>' — must be 'yes' or 'no'.
                    </sch:assert>
                </sch:rule>
            </sch:pattern>

            <sch:pattern>
                <sch:title>LOTAR Core Common: Attribute Rules</sch:title>
                <!--
                    BUG FOUND AND FIXED (2026-07-21): the original value used an XPath 2.0 sequence
                    constructor "('a', 'b', 'c')" for @keyword = $allowed-keywords membership
                    testing. This project's Schematron convention is queryBinding="xslt"
                    (XPath 1.0/XSLT 1.0) throughout — XPath 1.0 has no sequence/tuple type, so this
                    never compiled (confirmed: "Failed to compile the XPath expression" under
                    lxml's isoschematron). Rewritten using the standard XPath 1.0
                    delimited-string-membership idiom instead: a single '|'-delimited string, tested
                    with contains(). Same effective semantics, actually XPath-1.0-valid.
                -->
                <sch:let name="allowed-keywords" value="'|administrative.version|administrative.created-on|administrative.last-modified-date|administrative.created-by|administrative.author-organization|administrative.approver|administrative.approval-state|administrative.authorization|administrative.usage|administrative.model-dependency|administrative.model-hierarchy|administrative.abstraction-level|administrative.privacy-rules|administrative.specification-type-objectives|administrative.generation-tool|administrative.disposal-reason|administrative.size-bytes|administrative.submission-agreement-ref|administrative.archiving-required|legal.copyright|legal.license|legal.export-controls|legal.export-control-marking|legal.special-markings|dc.contributor|dc.coverage|dc.language|dc.publisher|dc.relation|dc.source|dc.subject|'"/>

                <sch:rule context="/srmd:SimulationResourceMetaData/stc:Classification[@type='org.lotar-international.lotar-core.common']/stc:ClassificationEntry">
                    <sch:assert test="contains($allowed-keywords, concat('|', @keyword, '|'))">
                        Unknown LOTAR Core (Common) classification attribute '<sch:value-of select="@keyword"/>'.
                    </sch:assert>
                </sch:rule>
            </sch:pattern>

            <sch:pattern>
                <sch:title>LOTAR Core Common: Dublin Core Repeatable-Keyword Rule (absorbed 2026-08-06)</sch:title>
                <!--
                    Ported verbatim from the retired SRMDLOTARCore_DublinCore.xsd, and — like every
                    other pattern in this file (see the BUG FOUND AND FIXED header note above) —
                    written with the literal Classification/@type and @keyword strings inlined
                    directly into the rule's context= attribute rather than via a sch:let variable,
                    since this toolchain (lxml's isoschematron/libxslt) does not support sch:let
                    variables inside a rule's context= match pattern. Every other keyword in this
                    file's whitelist is single-valued-per-entry (unchanged); dc.coverage,
                    dc.language, and dc.publisher are covered by the non-repeatable rule below;
                    dc.contributor, dc.relation, dc.source, and dc.subject are deliberately exempt
                    (carried over from Dublin Core's own repeatable elements — each can legitimately
                    occur more than once on the same resource).
                -->
                <sch:rule context="/srmd:SimulationResourceMetaData/stc:Classification[@type='org.lotar-international.lotar-core.common']/stc:ClassificationEntry[@keyword='dc.coverage' or @keyword='dc.language' or @keyword='dc.publisher']">
                    <sch:assert test="count(preceding-sibling::stc:ClassificationEntry[@keyword=current()/@keyword]) = 0">
                        The attribute '<sch:value-of select="@keyword"/>' occurred more than once (this keyword is not defined as repeatable).
                    </sch:assert>
                </sch:rule>
            </sch:pattern>
        </xs:appinfo>
    </xs:annotation>

</xs:schema>
